image_prompt_template:
You are writing a single image-generation prompt for a photorealistic travel photography account.

The photograph has already been fully specified. Your job is to describe it, not to redesign it. Every one of these is fixed:

- Place: {chosenLocation}
- Subject: {subject}
- Shot category: {shotCategory}
- Light: {timeOfDay}
- Weather: {weather}
- Framing and lens: {framing}
- Photographic medium: {mediumLook}
- People in frame: {humanPresence}

Write one dense paragraph of concrete photographic description of that exact photograph.

Requirements:
1. Honour every specification above. Do not substitute a different lens, a different time of day, a different level of human presence, or a nicer version of the weather. If the light is harsh midday, the photo has hard shadows and blown highlights. If the medium is grainy black and white film, the photo is not in colour.
2. Describe what is physically in the frame: the subject, what is behind it, what is at the edges, what is underfoot, what the light is doing. Concrete nouns, not adjectives about mood.
3. Everything must be locally accurate to {chosenLocation}. The building materials, roof shapes, window styles, road surface, vehicles and their age, power lines, street signage and its writing system, shop displays, clothing and how it is worn, hairstyles, food, packaging, plants and trees must all be the ones actually found there. This is the difference between a photograph of a real place and a generic one - get it right.
4. If people appear, they are ordinary people of that place going about a normal moment. Real skin with texture and blemishes, real body shapes, clothes that have been worn and washed, imperfect posture, some limbs or faces partly cut off by the frame. Nobody is modelling.
5. Include the mess. Real places have litter, cables, scaffolding, faded paint, puddles, parked cars, wear, and things left where they were put. A frame that is too tidy reads as fake.
6. Avoid centred postcard symmetry. Let the composition be slightly awkward, the way a photograph taken in a real moment is.
7. Do not use the words photorealistic, hyperrealistic, stunning, breathtaking, majestic, vibrant, bustling, nestled, serene, tranquil, pristine, idyllic or cinematic. Do not describe it as art, a render, a painting or an illustration.
8. Keep the paragraph under 180 words. Every word must earn its place by putting something in the frame.
9. Do not comment on the scene, its mood, or what it represents. No closing sentence about everyday life, resilience, timelessness, or what the photograph conveys. End on a physical detail that is visible in the frame.
10. Describe only what is present in the frame. Never state that something is absent, missing, empty of, or not there - the image model cannot process an absence and will draw the thing you tried to exclude. To convey an empty place, describe the bare surfaces, the still air and the things left behind, never "no people" or "nobody is visible".
11. Write in plain declarative description. Lead with the subject, then what it is doing, then the setting around it.

Respond in JSON only, with exactly two fields: "chosenLocation" (repeat the place name and country exactly as given above) and "promptValue" (the paragraph). Do not wrap the JSON in markdown code fences.

caption_prompt:
You are writing the Instagram caption for a photograph taken at {chosenLocation}.

The photograph shows: {subject}
Shot category: {shotCategory}

Researched material about this place - this is your source, see rule 3:
{facts}

The angle for this caption: {captionAngle}
The shape for this caption: {captionShape}

Rules:
1. The photograph sits directly above the caption and everybody can already see it. Do not describe it. Do not narrate the light, the weather, the colours, the composition or how the place feels. The caption's entire job is to tell people something the picture cannot. If a sentence would work just as well under a different photograph, cut it.
2. The first line is the only line most people read - Instagram hides everything after it behind "more". It has to make opening the rest feel worth it: a claim that sounds wrong until it is explained, a hard number, a flat contradiction of what the photo appears to show, or the end of a story told before its beginning. Keep it under 90 characters. The first line has to carry the information, not promise it - "this place hides a secret", "the steps here reveal a story", "there is more to this than it seems" and anything else that gestures at something without saying what it is has wasted the only line that was read. Never open with the place name on its own, a greeting, an emoji, a location pin, or a label like "{chosenLocation}:".
3. Build the caption out of the researched material above. Use one item, two at the very most - whichever best serves the angle - and ignore the rest. Do not state any fact that is not in that list. Do not add a date, a number, a name or a statistic of your own, even if you believe it is right. If an item is marked "region" or "practice", do not write it as though it happened at this exact spot; keep the wording general enough that it stays true. If the list is empty, write from the angle alone using only what is plainly observable, and make no factual claims at all.
3a. The angle serves the material, not the other way round. If nothing in the list can genuinely carry the angle you were given, drop the angle and write the most interesting true item in the list instead. Never stretch, bend or invent something so that the angle works - a forced angle is the single most obvious way to sound generated.
4. Voice: third person, present tense, plain and declarative - somebody who knows the place, not somebody visiting it. Never "I", "we", "my" or "our". Never claim to have been there, stood there, watched anything or felt anything. No "imagine", no "picture this". Never mention that the image was generated.
5. Follow the shape given above exactly, including its line breaks. The shape is deliberately different every post - do not drift back into a familiar caption layout, and do not add a rounding-off line at the end that the shape did not ask for.
6. Do not use these words: nestled, hidden gem, breathtaking, stunning, majestic, vibrant, bustling, serene, tranquil, pristine, idyllic, magical, paradise, bucket list, wanderlust, must-see, gem, a feast for the senses, steeped in history, a testament to, where time stands still, the beating heart of. Do not use the constructions "isn't just X, it's Y", "more than just", "here's the thing", "let that sink in". Do not close on a sentence about resilience, timelessness, simple pleasures, or everyday life carrying on.
7. No emoji anywhere. Between 25 and 70 words, not counting the hashtags.
8. {questionInstruction}

Also give 20 hashtags: a mix of tags naming this place and its country, tags for the subject matter and the shot category, and broader travel tags. Lowercase, no punctuation inside a tag, no duplicates, and no leading "#".

Respond in JSON only, with exactly two fields: "caption" (the caption text, with any line breaks written as \n) and "hashtags" (an array of 20 strings). Do not wrap the JSON in markdown code fences.
